Body Dysmorphic Disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Preoccupation with perceived physical flaws, Repetitive mirror checking/grooming, Distress over unobservable defects

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-013
Reason for Visit: Severe acute anxiety, skin excoriation, and suicidal ideation driven by obsessive preoccupation with perceived facial asymmetry.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted acute self-harm (severe facial picking/excoriation) driven by profound distress over perceived appearance flaws, culminating in threats of suicide. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandated to assess the physical damage to the face, evaluate suicide risk, and provide immediate psychological crisis intervention that could not safely wait until the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My nose is completely deformed and crooked. I tried to fix the skin but I ruined it, and I can't live looking like this."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been chronic but escalated acutely this evening. The patient reports a persistent preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance (specifically the shape of the nose and minor pores) that are entirely not observable by others. The patient engages in the performance of repetitive behaviors, such as spending 4 hours today mirror checking and excessive grooming/skin picking. The patient engages in constant mental acts, comparing their appearance with others. This causes significant distress and impairment, resulting in total avoidance of social situations due to appearance-related anxiety. The patient reports frequent pursuit of dermatological interventions without satisfaction, and camouflaging behavior using heavy makeup. Tonight, the severe distress over the unobservable defects led to acute excoriation of the facial skin and subsequent suicidal ideation stemming from body image distress.
Functional Status: Housebound; unable to attend work for three days due to perceived ugliness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of worsening self-mutilation (skin picking) and moderate risk of impulsive suicide.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/88 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Highly distressed, wearing a hat pulled low and a thick scarf indoors, avoiding eye contact.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular and respiratory systems unremarkable. Integumentary exam reveals multiple fresh, superficial excoriations and erythema over the nasal bridge and bilateral cheeks, secondary to active picking. No signs of deep soft tissue infection or cellulitis. Mental state examination reveals intense preoccupation with perceived physical flaws, repetitive mirror checking behaviors described, and immense distress over unobservable defects. Insight is extremely poor regarding the distorted self-image.
Environmental Observations: All mirrors in the living area are covered with towels.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Body Dysmorphic Disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute self-excoriation and suicidal ideation)
Differentials:

Delusional Disorder, Somatic Type - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient, when challenged intensely, can acknowledge that others might not see the defect, whereas a true delusion would be entirely fixed and unshakeable.

Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder - Body Dysmorphic Disorder is diagnosed instead because the obsessions and compulsions are restricted entirely to physical appearance and perceived defects.

Cellulitis (Facial) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the bilateral, diffuse nature of the superficial scratching without induration, severe warmth, or fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in acute psychological crisis, demonstrating self-mutilating behavior (severe skin excoriation) and expressing suicidal ideation secondary to profound body image distortion. Telephone triage appropriately identified the urgency of the self-harm and the suicide risk. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to clear the facial wounds for acute infection, definitively assess the immediate suicide risk, and provide pharmacological anxiolysis to break the compulsive skin-picking cycle. Delaying intervention would have resulted in deeper facial tissue damage and a highly elevated risk of suicide attempt.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Wounds cleaned with saline; topical antibiotic ointment applied. Prescribed a short course of oral Diazepam 2mg BD for 3 days to provide acute anxiolysis and interrupt the severe compulsive picking cycle. Expected investigation results: structured psychological assessment focusing on appearance preoccupation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "Mirror Fasting." Instruct the patient to keep the towels over the mirrors. If the urge to check the mirror arises, mandate a 10-minute delay where the patient must hold an ice cube in their hand. The intense sensory input of the cold distracts the nervous system, rewarding the delay with a decrease in the acute panic spike.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to specialized OCD/BDD psychology clinic.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours via Telehealth (to accommodate avoidance).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient experiences an uncontrollable urge to severely cut or mutilate their face, or if active suicidal planning occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute crisis management provided; BDD is a complex disorder requiring long-term SSRI and specialized therapy.
Clinical Course: The acute anxiety and picking will decrease with the sedative, but the underlying distorted perception remains chronic without intervention.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your face becomes hot, severely swollen, or if you develop a fever, seek medical care. If you feel you are going to seriously harm yourself tonight, call 000 immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain is currently sending false alarm signals about your appearance, causing immense panic. The medication will temporarily quiet that alarm so you can stop picking at your skin and let it heal.
Actionable Steps: Apply the ointment twice daily. Leave the mirrors covered. Use the ice cube technique to ride out the intense urges to check your face.

